.\" The line above is the preprocessor marker: this page has a .TS table, and
.\" without it man does not run tbl and the KEYS section renders as garbage.
.TH SPONUX 1 "2026-07-26" "sponux 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sponux \- Spotlight-like launcher for applications, files and arithmetic
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sponux
.RI [ OPTION ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sponux
is a keyboard launcher for X11.
It searches installed applications, a self-maintained index of filenames under
your home directory, and evaluates arithmetic expressions.
.PP
It runs as a single-instance resident daemon.
The first call starts it and shows the window; every later call toggles that
window, which takes about 20 ms because no interpreter is started \(em the
wrapper pokes the running daemon over D\-Bus.
Bind
.B sponux
to a key in your window manager or desktop; no hotkey daemon and no
window-manager rules are needed.
.PP
The window takes itself out of the window manager's hands entirely (X11
override-redirect) and places itself at the top-centre of whichever monitor
holds the pointer.
It claims the keyboard focus but never becomes the window manager's focused
window, so a tiling WM keeps its focus highlight where it was.
Under Wayland the placement is left to the compositor.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-daemon
Start the resident daemon without showing the window.
Intended for autostart at login: it moves the ~420 ms cold start out of the
first keypress.
Does nothing if a daemon is already running.
.TP
.BR \-\-autostart " [" on | off | status ]
Show, enable or disable starting the daemon when you log in, by writing or
removing
.IR ~/.config/autostart/sponux.desktop .
With no argument, reports the current state.
.TP
.B \-\-reindex
Rebuild the file index now and exit.
Rarely needed: the daemon watches every indexed directory with inotify and
rebuilds in full every 15 minutes as a safety net.
.TP
.B \-\-write-config
Write commented starter files to
.IR ~/.config/sponux/ .
Existing files are kept unless
.B \-\-force
is given, in which case the previous version is backed up to
.IR ~/.local/state/sponux/ .
.TP
.BI \-\-which " PATH"
Explain how
.I PATH
would be opened: its content type, which rule in
.I config.toml
matched, the exact command, and whether that command exists.
The mime types are not guessable \(em GIO reports a
.I .ts
file as Qt Linguist \(em so this is the honest way to write a rule.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Check
.IR config.toml :
that every configured command is on
.BR PATH ,
that the index roots exist, and that each include lies inside a root.
.SH KEYS
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
Type@search applications, files and arithmetic
Up / Down@move the selection
Enter@launch, open, or copy a calculator result
Ctrl+Enter@open the folder containing the selected file
Ctrl+C@copy the selected file's path
Shift+Enter@open with\[u2026], optionally remembering the choice
Escape@hide the window
Ctrl+Q@quit the daemon
.TE
.PP
The keys that apply to the selected result are shown under the results.
The four modifier actions and
.B Ctrl+Q
can be rebound in
.BR [keys] ;
see
.B FILES
below.
.SH QUERIES
A prefix restricts the search to one kind of result; a leading
.B /
or
.B ~/
means the query is a path rather than a search term.
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
\fBf:\fP \fBfile:\fP@files only
\fBa:\fP \fBapp:\fP@applications only
\fBc:\fP \fB=\fP@the calculator only, as in \fB=1/7\fP
\fB/\fP\[u2026] \fB~/\fP\[u2026]@that path, and completions of its last component
.TE
.PP
The prefixes are listed under the search field while it is empty and disappear
as soon as anything is typed.
.PP
Typing a path is the way past the index.
What the file search can find is limited by
.B [index]
in
.IR config.toml ,
so
.IR /usr/lib ,
.I /etc
and any excluded tree cannot be found by name however exactly it is spelled;
indexing everything is what makes results useless, so the escape hatch is to
name the path instead.
.B /etc/ho
completes to
.I /etc/hosts
with no index involved, a trailing slash lists a directory, and hidden entries
appear once the dot is typed, as in a shell.
A typed path always outranks every search result.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/sponux/config.toml
Behaviour: which application opens what, what the index covers, ranking.
Optional; edits apply the next time the window is opened, with no restart.
.PP
.B [window]
sets the card's width, how many results it shows,
.B position
(\fBtop\fP or \fBcenter\fP), whether losing focus dismisses it, and the typing
debounce.
.B [keys]
rebinds five actions \(em
.BR reveal ", " copy_path ", " open_with ", " close " and " quit
\(em in GTK accelerator syntax, the same as i3's
.BR bindsym :
.BR "<Ctrl>Return" ", " "<Shift><Alt>c" ", " F2 .
Typing, the arrow keys and Enter are not configurable, and Escape closes the
window whatever
.B close
says, so a mistake here cannot lock you out.
A binding is matched against the physical key, so
.B <Ctrl>c
keeps working under a non-Latin keyboard layout.
.TP
.I ~/.config/sponux/style.css
Appearance, layered on top of the bundled dark stylesheet.
Redefining a handful of
.B \-\-sponux\-*
variables in
.B :root
is the intended way to restyle it.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/sponux/index.db
The filename index. Safe to delete; it rebuilds.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sponux/usage.db
What you have opened, and when, for ranking.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/sponux/*.bak
The last version of a config file sponux replaced.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
.TQ
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
.TQ
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Honoured for all of the paths above.
.SH EXAMPLES
Bind it in i3:
.PP
.RS
.nf
bindsym $mod+d exec \-\-no\-startup\-id sponux
.fi
.RE
.PP
Unlike rofi, sponux does not need
.B \-\-release
on the binding: it claims the input focus rather than grabbing the keyboard,
so the window manager holding a grab during the key press does not interfere.
.PP
Start the daemon at login and see what a file would open with:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sponux \-\-autostart on
sponux \-\-which ~/.config/i3/config
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rofi (1),
.BR dmenu (1)
.PP
The full documentation is the README, installed as
.IR /usr/share/doc/sponux/README.md .
